  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through Tuesday)\nIssued at 325 AM EDT Wed Sep 30 2026\n\nA temporary return to the routine South Florida summertime pattern \nseems likely Friday onwards, as mid-level ridging and surface high \nmaintain their influence across the area through the weekend. \nEasterly flow and continued moisture advection will result in \nafternoon convection developing along the East Coast, then drifting \nwestward with the mean flow. \n\nConditions could transition back to a more wet and unsettled pattern \nonce again early next week with the potential development of another \ntrough over the eastern United States. This potential scenario could \nbring a cold front near the Florida peninsula sometime during the \nearly half of next week. The timing and intensity of these features \ncould determine when and where we get enhanced chances of rainfall.\nensemble members from the global Canadian ensemble model suggest \na deeper, more progressive trough, which would support enhanced \nrainfall chances early in the week. Meanwhile members from the \nEuropean and American ensemble models show a slightly slower, \npotentially weaker system which would favor rainfall later in the \nweek. For now, we left the generally bullish NBM forecast of \n70-80% PoPs Monday and Tuesday untouched, but we will continue to \nmonitor this forecast for any changes through the week.\n\nThere is increasing confidence that temperatures will increase back \ninto the upper 80s and low 90s areawide over the weekend, especially \nas easterly flow and moisture return increase.",
